      "dom_id": "question-48516",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "If a curve $C$ is given by $y={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate using the product rule. Let $u(x)={e}^{x}$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{x},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & ={{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)|}_{x=\\pi } \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-{e}^{\\pi }.$",
